   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Trade Union Labour Migration Policy, a guiding framework to the 51 sector unions affiliated to the Nigeria Labour Congress (NLC) was approved in February 2023 at the 18th Nigeria Labour Congress (NLC) Quadrennial National Delegates Conference, the highest policy making organ of the NLC, gathering over 2500 delegates in Abuja. The policy is anchored on the Nigerian National Labour Migration Policy (2015), Agenda 2030, and relevant international labour standards. Prior to the presentation of the Trade Union Labour Migration Policy to the NLC Delegates Conference, the policy was presented for discussion and recognition in a tripartite plus inter-institutional mechanism for policy discussion involving Nigeria Employers&apos; Consultative Council (NECA), Ministry of Humanitarian Affairs and Social Welfare (NCFRMI), and the International Labour Migration Desk (ILMD) housed in the Federal Ministry of Labour and Employment (FMLE) and Civil Society Organisations (CSOs).

The trade union Labour Migration Policy will contribute towards enhancing organisation of trade union collaboration, providing policy direction, and influencing a rights-based labour migration governance in Nigeria.
 The ILO provided support to the NLC and the Trade Union Congress of Nigeria (TUC) from September to December 2022 to draft a Trade Union Labour Migration Policy. ILO organised a policy drafting best practice workshop organised in January 2023 in Abuja.</narrative>
   </description>
